1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,073
In the early 2000s, a small Nordic country
became a place of pilgrimage.

2
00:00:05,203 --> 00:00:09,543
Education ministers, school principals and
consultants flew to Helsinki

3
00:00:09,563 --> 00:00:11,987
to find out what Finland knew that they
didn't.

4
00:00:12,117 --> 00:00:16,497
The national education agency alone has
hosted more than sixteen thousand

5
00:00:16,517 --> 00:00:17,281
visitors.

6
00:00:17,411 --> 00:00:20,163
Eventually, Finland started charging
admission.

7
00:00:20,293 --> 00:00:24,433
A school visit now costs one thousand four
hundred and eighty-five euros.

8
00:00:24,563 --> 00:00:25,993
Plus VAT.

9
00:00:26,493 --> 00:00:28,285
They came to see a miracle.

10
00:00:28,415 --> 00:00:34,475
In 2006, Finnish fifteen-year-olds scored
548 points in mathematics

11
00:00:34,495 --> 00:00:36,629
on the world's largest school test.

12
00:00:36,759 --> 00:00:40,280
For reformers everywhere, that number
became a destination.

13
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:42,765
Now watch what happened next.

14
00:00:43,015 --> 00:00:44,935
541.

15
00:00:45,185 --> 00:00:47,106
519.

16
00:00:47,356 --> 00:00:49,255
511.

17
00:00:49,505 --> 00:00:51,319
507.

18
00:00:51,569 --> 00:00:53,554
484.

19
00:00:53,684 --> 00:00:56,949
And in the results published in September
2026 ...

20
00:00:57,199 --> 00:00:59,247
469.

21
00:00:59,747 --> 00:01:02,201
That is a fall of 79 points.

22
00:01:02,331 --> 00:01:07,516
By the OECD's own rough yardstick, about
22 points is a year of learning.

23
00:01:07,646 --> 00:01:10,026
So today's Finnish fifteen-year-olds are,

24
00:01:10,046 --> 00:01:14,772
very roughly, three years of maths behind
the teenagers of 2006.

25
00:01:15,272 --> 00:01:18,132
The usual explanations are phones, the
pandemic,

26
00:01:18,152 --> 00:01:19,241
and immigration.

27
00:01:19,371 --> 00:01:22,102
Each of them is either wrong or
incomplete.

28
00:01:22,232 --> 00:01:24,259
The better answer is stranger.

29
00:01:24,389 --> 00:01:27,227
The world may have copied Finland for the
wrong reasons.

30
00:01:27,357 --> 00:01:31,155
And Finland, it turns out, may have made
the same mistake about itself.

31
00:01:31,285 --> 00:01:33,705
Let's test every suspect against the data,

32
00:01:33,725 --> 00:01:35,531
and see which ones survive.

33
00:01:37,981 --> 00:01:39,539
First, the scoreboard.

34
00:01:39,669 --> 00:01:43,979
PISA is the OECD's Programme for
International Student Assessment.

35
00:01:44,189 --> 00:01:46,409
Roughly every three years since 2000,

36
00:01:46,429 --> 00:01:48,689
it tests fifteen-year-olds in reading,

37
00:01:48,709 --> 00:01:50,446
mathematics and science.

38
00:01:50,576 --> 00:01:54,596
The 2025 round covered ninety-one
countries and economies,

39
00:01:54,616 --> 00:01:57,745
and about seven hundred and sixty thousand
students.

40
00:01:57,875 --> 00:02:01,630
It's not a perfect instrument, and we'll
get to its limits later.

41
00:02:02,130 --> 00:02:04,989
PISA doesn't ask students to recite a
syllabus.

42
00:02:05,119 --> 00:02:08,659
It asks whether they can use what they
know on problems they haven't seen

43
00:02:08,679 --> 00:02:09,280
before.

44
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:12,440
That makes it hard to cram for, and easy
to headline.

45
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:15,728
A bad PISA result can sink an education
minister.

46
00:02:15,858 --> 00:02:18,589
A good one can launch a consulting
industry.

47
00:02:19,089 --> 00:02:23,143
On the PISA scale, a gap of ten or fifteen
points is already meaningful.

48
00:02:23,273 --> 00:02:27,157
The OECD reckons about twenty-two points
is a year of school.

49
00:02:27,287 --> 00:02:29,506
A gap of eighty is a different world.

50
00:02:31,956 --> 00:02:35,696
When the first PISA results came out in
December 2001,

51
00:02:35,716 --> 00:02:38,016
Finland was top of the world in reading,

52
00:02:38,036 --> 00:02:40,490
with 546 points.

53
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:43,960
By 2006, the numbers were even more
striking:

54
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:50,280
547 in reading, 548 in maths, and 563

55
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:51,202
in science.

56
00:02:51,702 --> 00:02:55,762
The visitors found a system that looked
almost designed to embarrass everyone

57
00:02:55,782 --> 00:02:57,036
else's reforms.

58
00:02:57,166 --> 00:02:59,684
Children didn't start school until seven.

59
00:02:59,814 --> 00:03:04,194
There was no national standardised test
until the very end of upper secondary

60
00:03:04,214 --> 00:03:04,743
school.

61
00:03:04,873 --> 00:03:07,177
School inspectors had been abolished.

62
00:03:07,307 --> 00:03:10,593
And every primary school teacher needed a
master's degree.

63
00:03:11,093 --> 00:03:13,113
Here was a country that tested less,

64
00:03:13,133 --> 00:03:15,513
started later and trusted teachers more,

65
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:16,683
and it still won.

66
00:03:16,813 --> 00:03:19,673
For reformers who were tired of exams and
league tables,

67
00:03:19,693 --> 00:03:21,337
it was the perfect story.

68
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:24,807
Finland became shorthand for a whole
philosophy of schooling,

69
00:03:24,827 --> 00:03:28,017
quoted by people who had never sat in a
Finnish classroom.

70
00:03:28,517 --> 00:03:30,821
None of those features was invented.

71
00:03:30,951 --> 00:03:32,189
They were real.

72
00:03:32,319 --> 00:03:34,688
The problem is a single word: because.

73
00:03:34,818 --> 00:03:37,805
Finland had few tests, and Finland scored
highly.

74
00:03:37,935 --> 00:03:42,096
The world concluded that Finland scored
highly because it had few tests.

75
00:03:42,596 --> 00:03:46,074
But think about who actually sat the test
in 2006.

76
00:03:46,204 --> 00:03:49,405
They were fifteen, so they were born
around 1991.

77
00:03:49,535 --> 00:03:53,055
They started school at seven, around 1998.

78
00:03:53,185 --> 00:03:56,245
Much of the freedom the visitors admired
had only been written into the

79
00:03:56,265 --> 00:04:01,725
rules in the 1990s, when inspections and
centrally approved materials disappeared

80
00:04:01,745 --> 00:04:04,557
and schools began writing their own
curricula.

81
00:04:04,687 --> 00:04:06,671
Rules can change in a year.

82
00:04:06,801 --> 00:04:09,021
Classrooms change over decades.

83
00:04:09,151 --> 00:04:12,731
Many of the teachers in front of those
high scorers had trained and worked

84
00:04:12,751 --> 00:04:16,170
for years in an older, far more
centralised system.

85
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:18,114
Keep that date in mind.

86
00:04:18,244 --> 00:04:19,781
We'll come back to it.

87
00:04:22,231 --> 00:04:25,197
Here is Finland's maths score, round by
round.

88
00:04:25,447 --> 00:04:28,583
544 in 2003.

89
00:04:28,713 --> 00:04:32,319
A peak of 548 in 2006.

90
00:04:32,449 --> 00:04:34,909
Then down in every single round after
that,

91
00:04:34,929 --> 00:04:38,103
to 469 in 2025.

92
00:04:38,603 --> 00:04:40,609
Reading tells the same story.

93
00:04:40,739 --> 00:04:46,519
From a peak of 547 in 2006 to 474 today,

94
00:04:46,539 --> 00:04:49,593
level with Italy, in joint seventeenth
place.

95
00:04:49,723 --> 00:04:54,423
Science has held up best: 563 in 2006,

96
00:04:54,443 --> 00:04:57,169
504 now, twelfth in the world.

97
00:04:57,299 --> 00:04:59,839
The fall is real, but so is the altitude.

98
00:04:59,969 --> 00:05:04,065
Finland is still above the OECD average in
all three subjects.

99
00:05:04,565 --> 00:05:06,145
Just not by much.

100
00:05:06,275 --> 00:05:11,651
In maths, the OECD average in 2025 was
463.

101
00:05:11,781 --> 00:05:14,385
Finland scored 469.

102
00:05:14,515 --> 00:05:18,415
The country that the world once flew in to
study now sits six points from

103
00:05:18,435 --> 00:05:19,678
the middle of the table.

104
00:05:19,808 --> 00:05:22,177
In reading, the gap is thirteen points.

105
00:05:22,307 --> 00:05:24,953
Only science still looks like the old
Finland.

106
00:05:25,453 --> 00:05:27,608
And Finland is not falling alone.

107
00:05:27,738 --> 00:05:33,358
Across the OECD, average reading scores
dropped 28 points between 2015 and

108
00:05:33,378 --> 00:05:37,104
2025, which is about a year and a half of
learning.

109
00:05:37,234 --> 00:05:39,048
Maths dropped 22.

110
00:05:39,178 --> 00:05:44,918
Between 2018 and 2022, the OECD average in
maths fell fifteen points,

111
00:05:44,938 --> 00:05:46,880
the largest drop on record.

112
00:05:47,050 --> 00:05:49,955
Part of Finland's story is simply
everyone's story.

113
00:05:50,455 --> 00:05:51,927
But only part.

114
00:05:52,057 --> 00:05:54,895
An average can hide a lot, so let's split
it open.

115
00:05:55,025 --> 00:05:59,405
In the early 2000s, about seven per cent
of Finnish students scored below

116
00:05:59,425 --> 00:06:01,191
the baseline level in maths.

117
00:06:01,321 --> 00:06:04,031
By 2022 it was one in four.

118
00:06:04,161 --> 00:06:08,861
Since 2015, the share of low performers
has risen by seventeen percentage

119
00:06:08,881 --> 00:06:11,586
points in maths and fifteen in reading.

120
00:06:11,716 --> 00:06:16,026
In reading today, more than one Finnish
student in four is below the baseline.

121
00:06:16,156 --> 00:06:18,482
Among boys, it's about one in three.

122
00:06:18,982 --> 00:06:20,796
The top has thinned out too.

123
00:06:20,926 --> 00:06:25,186
In 2003, more than twenty-three per cent
of Finnish students reached the

124
00:06:25,206 --> 00:06:26,772
highest levels in maths.

125
00:06:26,902 --> 00:06:32,407
In 2025, seven per cent did, which is now
below the OECD average.

126
00:06:32,537 --> 00:06:34,735
Finland didn't just lose its floor.

127
00:06:34,865 --> 00:06:36,764
It lost its ceiling as well.

128
00:06:37,264 --> 00:06:38,907
Now bring in the neighbour.

129
00:06:39,037 --> 00:06:41,697
Estonia sits just across the Gulf of
Finland,

130
00:06:41,717 --> 00:06:44,457
and its language is a close relative of
Finnish.

131
00:06:44,587 --> 00:06:49,558
In 2006, Estonia trailed Finland in maths
by thirty-three points.

132
00:06:49,848 --> 00:06:54,748
By 2012, the two were level: 521 to 519,

133
00:06:54,768 --> 00:06:56,259
a statistical tie.

134
00:06:56,389 --> 00:06:59,057
Since then, the lines have only moved
apart.

135
00:06:59,557 --> 00:07:05,936
In 2025, Estonia scored 508 in maths
against Finland's 469.

136
00:07:06,146 --> 00:07:11,326
It's ahead in science, 527 to 504, and in
reading,

137
00:07:11,346 --> 00:07:14,792
499 to 474.

138
00:07:14,922 --> 00:07:18,571
Estonia is now Europe's best performer in
maths and science.

139
00:07:18,701 --> 00:07:22,264
To be fair, Estonia's scores have slipped
since 2018 too.

140
00:07:22,394 --> 00:07:24,933
It's falling more slowly, and from higher
up.

141
00:07:25,433 --> 00:07:29,613
And at the top of the OECD table, there's
a name that rarely came up in

142
00:07:29,633 --> 00:07:30,831
the Finland story.

143
00:07:30,961 --> 00:07:35,821
In 2025, Japan ranked first among OECD
members in reading,

144
00:07:35,841 --> 00:07:39,901
maths and science, the first time it has
done so since PISA began.

145
00:07:40,031 --> 00:07:43,931
Back in 2022, when maths scores fell
almost everywhere,

146
00:07:43,951 --> 00:07:47,797
Japan and Korea were the only OECD members
that improved.

147
00:07:47,927 --> 00:07:50,935
So is the lesson simply, "be more like
East Asia"?

148
00:07:51,065 --> 00:07:52,495
Hold that thought.

149
00:07:54,945 --> 00:07:58,231
There are six popular explanations for
Finland's decline.

150
00:07:58,361 --> 00:08:01,263
A good explanation has to account for
three things.

151
00:08:01,393 --> 00:08:02,866
Why Finland fell.

152
00:08:02,996 --> 00:08:05,834
Why it fell further than most of the OECD.

153
00:08:05,964 --> 00:08:09,549
And why it started when it did, after
2006.

154
00:08:09,679 --> 00:08:11,539
Let's take the suspects one at a time,

155
00:08:11,559 --> 00:08:13,093
and give each a verdict.

156
00:08:13,593 --> 00:08:15,577
Suspect one: immigration.

157
00:08:15,707 --> 00:08:19,887
Finland has become more diverse, and
immigrant-background students do score

158
00:08:19,907 --> 00:08:21,020
lower on average.

159
00:08:21,150 --> 00:08:23,210
But if immigration explained the fall,

160
00:08:23,230 --> 00:08:25,865
the scores of everyone else would have
held steady.

161
00:08:25,995 --> 00:08:27,148
They didn't.

162
00:08:27,278 --> 00:08:30,991
Results fell among students with and
without a migrant background.

163
00:08:31,121 --> 00:08:34,581
In maths, immigrant-background students
were roughly where they had been

164
00:08:34,601 --> 00:08:35,772
in 2012.

165
00:08:35,902 --> 00:08:40,041
It was everyone else who fell, and the gap
between the groups actually narrowed.

166
00:08:40,171 --> 00:08:42,092
Verdict: not the main cause.

167
00:08:42,592 --> 00:08:45,131
Suspect two: phones and the pandemic.

168
00:08:45,261 --> 00:08:50,481
The OECD's 2025 report finds that students
who use devices heavily,

169
00:08:50,501 --> 00:08:54,073
or for leisure at school, tend to score
noticeably lower.

170
00:08:54,243 --> 00:08:58,343
More than one student in four says
classmates are distracted by devices

171
00:08:58,363 --> 00:09:00,006
in most science lessons.

172
00:09:00,136 --> 00:09:02,931
That's a correlation, not proof, but it's
a strong one.

173
00:09:03,061 --> 00:09:04,683
The problem is timing.

174
00:09:04,813 --> 00:09:09,993
Finland's slide began after 2006, more
than a decade before anyone had heard

175
00:09:10,013 --> 00:09:11,257
of COVID-19.

176
00:09:11,387 --> 00:09:14,865
And phones are everywhere, including in
Estonia and Japan.

177
00:09:14,995 --> 00:09:17,470
Verdict: an accelerant, not the origin.

178
00:09:17,970 --> 00:09:19,699
Suspect three: reading.

179
00:09:19,829 --> 00:09:24,609
Finland's own education agency says that
reading used to be a popular pastime,

180
00:09:24,629 --> 00:09:27,723
and that this habit held up results across
the board.

181
00:09:27,853 --> 00:09:30,691
Social media and gaming now take that
time.

182
00:09:30,821 --> 00:09:35,041
The OECD found that the share of Finnish
students who read for enjoyment

183
00:09:35,061 --> 00:09:40,699
fell between 2000 and 2009, faster than
the OECD average.

184
00:09:40,829 --> 00:09:43,027
Reading isn't just one subject.

185
00:09:43,157 --> 00:09:47,317
Every maths and science question in PISA
is also a reading test.

186
00:09:47,447 --> 00:09:51,032
A student who struggles to read the
problem never gets to the maths.

187
00:09:51,162 --> 00:09:53,382
It would also fit the shape of the
decline,

188
00:09:53,402 --> 00:09:56,302
which has been concentrated among weaker
students and,

189
00:09:56,322 --> 00:09:57,989
in reading, among boys.

190
00:09:58,119 --> 00:10:00,360
Verdict: a likely contributor.

191
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:03,207
Suspect four: student-led learning.

192
00:10:03,497 --> 00:10:08,437
In 2014, Finland published a new core curriculum built around cross-
disciplinary,

193
00:10:08,457 --> 00:10:10,165
phenomenon-based learning.

194
00:10:10,295 --> 00:10:13,239
Schools introduced it from August 2016.

195
00:10:13,369 --> 00:10:16,079
It's a tempting culprit, but look at the
calendar.

196
00:10:16,209 --> 00:10:19,069
Scores had been falling for a decade
before it arrived,

197
00:10:19,089 --> 00:10:21,394
so it can't have started the decline.

198
00:10:21,524 --> 00:10:25,984
The 2025 test-takers are roughly the first
to have had it for their entire

199
00:10:26,004 --> 00:10:27,007
school career.

200
00:10:27,137 --> 00:10:30,402
Whether it deepened the fall is still
argued over in Finland.

201
00:10:30,532 --> 00:10:32,581
Verdict: possible, not proven.

202
00:10:33,081 --> 00:10:35,193
Suspect five: inequality.

203
00:10:35,323 --> 00:10:40,316
The Finnish government's own summary of
2025 says the same thing three ways.

204
00:10:40,446 --> 00:10:42,025
More low performers.

205
00:10:42,155 --> 00:10:43,820
Fewer top performers.

206
00:10:43,950 --> 00:10:45,913
Wider gaps between students.

207
00:10:46,043 --> 00:10:47,836
That's true, and it matters.

208
00:10:47,966 --> 00:10:50,121
But it's less a cause than a description.

209
00:10:50,251 --> 00:10:52,471
It tells us where the damage landed.

210
00:10:52,601 --> 00:10:54,735
It doesn't tell us what did the damage.

211
00:10:55,235 --> 00:10:58,094
Which brings us to suspect six: the
legacy.

212
00:10:58,384 --> 00:11:02,844
In 2015, the economist Gabriel Heller
Sahlgren published a report called

213
00:11:02,864 --> 00:11:04,305
Real Finnish Lessons.

214
00:11:04,435 --> 00:11:06,526
His argument was uncomfortable.

215
00:11:06,656 --> 00:11:11,036
Finland's rise, he wrote, began before
most of its celebrated reforms could

216
00:11:11,056 --> 00:11:14,849
take effect, and its decline began soon
after they took hold.

217
00:11:14,979 --> 00:11:17,039
What produced the miracle was older:

218
00:11:17,059 --> 00:11:19,719
a hierarchical, teacher-led classroom
culture,

219
00:11:19,739 --> 00:11:22,211
and deep historical and cultural roots.

220
00:11:22,711 --> 00:11:26,936
Remember 1998, the year our peak test-
takers started school?

221
00:11:27,066 --> 00:11:29,200
We can test the timing ourselves.

222
00:11:29,330 --> 00:11:32,590
Take every Finnish maths score and slide
it back eight years,

223
00:11:32,610 --> 00:11:35,155
to roughly when those students started
school.

224
00:11:35,325 --> 00:11:37,504
Now the chart tells a different story.

225
00:11:37,634 --> 00:11:40,974
The best results belong to children who
entered school in the mid-to-late

226
00:11:40,994 --> 00:11:42,157
1990s.

227
00:11:42,287 --> 00:11:46,277
The decline begins with the cohorts who
started in the early 2000s.

228
00:11:46,407 --> 00:11:50,419
And the 2016 curriculum only arrives at
the very end of the line.

229
00:11:50,549 --> 00:11:52,683
This doesn't prove the legacy theory.

230
00:11:52,813 --> 00:11:55,459
Relabelling an axis never proves anything.

231
00:11:55,589 --> 00:11:58,896
But of all six suspects, this one fits the
timing best.

232
00:12:01,346 --> 00:12:04,141
So should everyone just copy Japan
instead?

233
00:12:04,271 --> 00:12:05,680
Not so fast.

234
00:12:05,810 --> 00:12:10,590
In the same 2025 survey, sixty-three per
cent of Japanese students said

235
00:12:10,610 --> 00:12:12,787
they were curious about many different
things.

236
00:12:12,917 --> 00:12:15,691
The OECD average was seventy-three.

237
00:12:15,821 --> 00:12:19,881
Forty-eight per cent said they put in
extra effort when work gets difficult,

238
00:12:19,901 --> 00:12:22,627
against an OECD average of sixty.

239
00:12:22,757 --> 00:12:25,217
Self-reported answers vary with culture,

240
00:12:25,237 --> 00:12:27,728
and modesty is part of the culture here.

241
00:12:27,858 --> 00:12:31,656
But these are not the numbers of a system
that has solved education.

242
00:12:32,156 --> 00:12:36,316
Critics like the education scholar Yong
Zhao have spent years arguing that

243
00:12:36,336 --> 00:12:39,016
PISA measures a narrow slice of what
schools do,

244
00:12:39,036 --> 00:12:43,698
and that test-driven systems can produce
high scores while crushing creativity.

245
00:12:43,828 --> 00:12:47,647
You don't have to agree with him
completely to accept the basic point.

246
00:12:47,777 --> 00:12:51,789
A test of fifteen-year-olds on one day is
not a verdict on a nation.

247
00:12:52,289 --> 00:12:54,060
And perspective matters.

248
00:12:54,190 --> 00:12:58,116
Finland is still above the OECD average in
every subject.

249
00:12:58,246 --> 00:13:00,444
It's twelfth in the world in science.

250
00:13:00,574 --> 00:13:04,794
Sliding from the top of the reading table
to joint seventeenth is a national

251
00:13:04,814 --> 00:13:06,335
crisis in Helsinki.

252
00:13:06,465 --> 00:13:09,025
In most capitals, it would be a press
release.

253
00:13:11,475 --> 00:13:12,884
So what happened?

254
00:13:13,014 --> 00:13:15,873
Finland's success in the 2000s was real.

255
00:13:16,003 --> 00:13:18,143
But the world, and in time Finland itself,

256
00:13:18,163 --> 00:13:19,759
misread what caused it.

257
00:13:19,889 --> 00:13:22,669
Visitors saw the visible features: the
late start,

258
00:13:22,689 --> 00:13:24,433
the missing tests, the freedom.

259
00:13:24,563 --> 00:13:28,423
They missed the invisible ones: a
generation that read for pleasure,

260
00:13:28,443 --> 00:13:32,159
and classrooms where teachers taught the
basics carefully and directly.

261
00:13:32,289 --> 00:13:34,615
The visible features could be exported.

262
00:13:34,745 --> 00:13:36,885
The invisible ones couldn't, and at home,

263
00:13:36,905 --> 00:13:38,351
they quietly faded.

264
00:13:38,851 --> 00:13:41,710
That's the real lesson, and it isn't about
Finland.

265
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:43,846
It's about how we read success.

266
00:13:43,976 --> 00:13:46,750
We tend to credit whatever is easiest to
see.

267
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:49,980
So the next time a headline announces an
education miracle,

268
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,531
ask three questions.

269
00:13:51,661 --> 00:13:53,987
When were these students actually taught?

270
00:13:54,117 --> 00:13:56,315
What changed before the scores did?

271
00:13:56,445 --> 00:13:58,771
And what's happening to the bottom
quarter?

272
00:13:58,901 --> 00:14:01,561
If a story can't answer those, it's a
brochure,

273
00:14:01,581 --> 00:14:02,998
not an explanation.

274
00:14:03,128 --> 00:14:06,868
Finland spent twenty years being the
answer to a question nobody had asked

275
00:14:06,888 --> 00:14:07,651
carefully.

276
00:14:10,101 --> 00:14:13,025
Finland didn't fall because it stopped
being Finland.

277
00:14:13,155 --> 00:14:16,825
It fell, at least partly, because it
started believing the brochure.

278
00:14:17,325 --> 00:14:19,352
So here's my question for you.

279
00:14:19,482 --> 00:14:22,597
What did your school get right that no
ranking would ever measure?

